{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[40,-62]},"properties":{"name":"Humpback whale · Antarctica to Mozambique & Madagascar","Species":"Humpback whale (Megaptera novaeangliae)","Population":"Breeding Stock C (SW Indian Ocean)","Feeding / summer ground":"Southern Ocean, 10°E–60°E","Breeding / winter ground":"Mozambique coast, Madagascar, Comoros","Distance (km)":"~5,000 one way","Timing":"Feed Dec–Apr; breed Jul–Oct","Abundance":"Several thousand","Status":"Recovering","Confidence":"Well documented","Notes":"Two main branches: one along the African mainland into the Mozambique Channel, one up the east coast of Madagascar to Sainte-Marie and Antongil Bay."}}
{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[158,-64],[156,-48],[154,-36],[154.2,-28],[153.4,-24.5],[151,-21],[149.8,-20]]},"properties":{"name":"Humpback whale · Antarctica to the Great Barrier Reef","Leg":"Migration route"}}
{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[158,-64]},"properties":{"name":"Humpback whale · Antarctica to the Great Barrier Reef","Species":"Humpback whale (Megaptera novaeangliae)","Population":"Breeding Stock E1 (East Australia)","Feeding / summer ground":"Southern Ocean south of New Zealand & Tasmania","Breeding / winter ground":"Great Barrier Reef; Hervey Bay rest stop","Distance (km)":"~5,000 one way","Timing":"North May–Aug; south Aug–Nov, mothers stopping in Hervey Bay","Abundance":"~35,000–40,000","Status":"Recovered","Confidence":"Well documented","Notes":"The so-called humpback highway along the New South Wales coast. Songs from this population often spread eastward across the South Pacific."}}
{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[100,-64],[105,-45],[112,-33],[112.5,-24],[113.5,-21.5],[118,-19],[123.6,-15.2]]},"properties":{"name":"Humpback whale · Antarctica to the Kimberley","Leg":"Migration route"}}
{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[100,-64]},"properties":{"name":"Humpback whale · Antarctica to the Kimberley","Species":"Humpback whale (Megaptera novaeangliae)","Population":"Breeding Stock D (West Australia)","Feeding / summer ground":"Southern Ocean, 80°E–130°E","Breeding / winter ground":"Camden Sound, Kimberley coast","Distance (km)":"~5,500 one way","Timing":"North Jun–Aug past Ningaloo; south Aug–Nov via Exmouth Gulf & Shark Bay","Abundance":"~30,000+","Status":"Recovered","Confidence":"Well documented","Notes":"Probably the largest humpback population on Earth. Exmouth Gulf and Shark Bay are key resting areas for mothers and calves heading south."}}
