{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-124.8,42]},"properties":{"name":"Blue whale · California to the Costa Rica Dome","Species":"Blue whale (Balaenoptera musculus)","Population":"Eastern North Pacific","Feeding / summer ground":"California Current, Oregon to Baja","Breeding / winter ground":"Costa Rica Dome; Gulf of California","Distance (km)":"~4,500 one way","Timing":"Feed Jun–Oct off California; winter Nov–Apr in the tropics","Abundance":"~1,900","Status":"Endangered (US ESA)","Confidence":"Well documented","Notes":"The Costa Rica Dome is an upwelling hotspot where blue whales breed and also feed year-round, which is unusual for a baleen whale breeding ground."}}
